Methods. Western blot and immunoprecipitation tech- 
niques for identification of the 128-kd protein, amphiphy- 
sin, have been reported recently.11J3 Rat tissues were fixed 
by transcardiac perfusion. Immunoperoxidase staining of 
frozen tissue sections was carried out as previously de- 
scribed.15J6 Patient sera was diluted l:lO, and 4% formal- 
dehyde was used as fixative. 
Autoantibodies against GAD were determined with im- 
muno~ytochemistry~ and radioligand techniques. The GAD 
radioligand assay was performed with both the recombi- 
nant 65- and 67-kd isoforms of human GAD (GAD65 
and GAD671 as recently reported.17 In the second GAD 
Antibody Proficiency Program, the assay achieved 100% 
sensitivity and 100% specifity. As compared with immuno- 
cytochemistry testing, this radioligand assay identifies ele- 
vated GAD antibody levels in about 50% more patients 
with the clinical diagnosis SMS.lS 
Results. Although immunocytochemistry revealed no 
anti-GAD aut~antibodies,~J~ retrospective investigation of 
stored serum samples with the radioligand as~ay~~J~ 
yielded levels of autoantibodies directed against GAD65 
that increased successively from 5 arbitrary units (AU) on 
second admission to 120 AU 2 years later (normal, <7 AU). 
In contrast, autoantibody levels against GAD67 constantly 
remained below the normal limit (6 AU). 
With Western blot and immunoprecipitation tech- 
niques, autoantibodies against amphiphysin, a neuronal 
128-kd protein, were detected in the patient’s serum and 
CSF.11J3 Using Western blots on rat brain homogenate, the 
serum of our patient recognized a band of 128 kd that 
precisely comigrated with a band recognized by both rabbit 
antibodies against chicken amphiphysin and rabbit serum 
